%I A399236 #118 Oct 01 2026 14:35:22
%S A399236 1,8,6,2,13,3,4,9,12,19,5,7,24,16,39,17,25,20,42,32,26,10,27,33,14,18,
%T A399236 11,31,41,34,15,35,63,51,40,30,59,50,62,53,28,54,43,21,44,52,61,49,23,
%U A399236 29,58,71,85,74,64,36,65,75,86,72,83,48,22,47,82,97,60,73
%N A399236 Squares visited by knight moves on a diagonally numbered Q1 board and always taking the available unvisited square with the shortest distance to the origin, while in the case of a tie the square with smaller row index is preferred. Starting square is labeled 1.
%C A399236 Board is numbered as follows:
%C A399236    1  2  4  7 11 16 ...
%C A399236    3  5  8 12 17 ...
%C A399236    6  9 13 18 ...
%C A399236   10 14 19 ...
%C A399236   15 20 ...
%C A399236   21 ...
%C A399236   ...
%C A399236 The knight's tour starts at position (1,1), the first square of the tour. The knight moves in the open field spanned by the positive x- and y-axis; i.e., the first quadrant Q1.
%C A399236 In case the knight has a choice between two squares, which have equal distance to the origin, the square with its row index being smaller than the row index of the alternative square is chosen to break the tie.
%C A399236 The knight becomes trapped after 416 moves at position (1, 21).
%H A399236 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A399236/b399236.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..417</a>
%H A399236 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A399236/a399236.svg">Illustration of the path</a>.
%H A399236 M. F. Hasler, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/Knight_tours">Knight tours</a>, OEIS wiki, Nov. 2019.
%H A399236 N. J. A. Sloane and Brady Haran, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGQe8waGJ4w">The Trapped Knight</a>, Numberphile video (2019).
%H A399236 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A399236/a399236.csv.txt">Array after knight was trapped as csv-file</a>.
%H A399236 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A399236/a399236.py.txt">Python program</a>.
%e A399236 Board after 12 steps: (0's are unvisited squares).
%e A399236   1   4   7  12 ...
%e A399236   6  11   2   9 ...
%e A399236   3   8   5   0 ...
%e A399236   0   0  10   0 ...
%e A399236   ...
%e A399236 a(1) = 1 because 1 is at position (1,1) which is numbered 1.
%e A399236 a(2) = 8 because 2 is at position (2,3) which is numbered 8. In this case, the tiebreaking rule chooses (2,3) over the other available candidate (3,2).
%e A399236 a(3) = 6 because 3 is at position (3,1) which is numbered 6.
%e A399236 a(12) = 7 because 12 is at position (1,4) which is numbered 7. In this case, the tiebreaking rule chooses (1,4) over the other available candidate (4,1).
